course: dictionary
lesson: 13518
title: Words that get in the way
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An impediment is something that gets in the way and makes it harder to go forward. Look at the context: «The heavy snow was an impediment to our walk.» The snow slowed us down, so an impediment is a thing that blocks or slows you.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «The heavy snow was a thing that slowed our walk.» It still makes sense, so you have the right idea.
explain: Some impediments are about the body. A speech impediment is a difficulty in speaking, such as a stammer or a lisp. «Ben has a speech impediment, so he takes his time when he talks.» Speak kindly about people with impediments: they are not less clever, they just find some things harder.
fact: An impediment is something that gets in the way or makes progress hard.
fact: «Impediment» is a noun: one impediment, two or more impediments.
fact: A speech impediment is a difficulty in speaking, such as a stammer or a lisp.
fact: A stammer is when the sounds at the start of a word get stuck or repeated; a lisp is when s and z sound a bit like th.
fact: In a wedding, an impediment is a legal reason two people cannot marry, but you will meet that meaning later.
exercise: «The fallen tree was an impediment to the cars on the road.» What is an impediment here? | A type of cloud | A fast car | *Something that blocks the way | A kind driver
exercise: «Ben has a speech impediment, so he takes his time when he talks.» What does speech impediment mean? | A loud voice | A song in class | *A difficulty in speaking | A story he likes to tell
exercise: Find and copy the word that means something that gets in the way, from: «The thick mud was an impediment to our bikes.» | bikes | *impediment | mud | thick
test: Mia says, «The rain is an impediment to our picnic.» What does impediment mean here? | A sunny seat | A picnic basket | A rainbow | *Something in the way of our picnic
test: «My sister has a stammer, but it is not an impediment to her being brilliant at maths.» What does this sentence tell us? | She cannot do maths | The stammer makes her brilliant at talking | *The stammer does not stop her being clever | Stammer is a kind of song
exam: Which sentence uses impediment correctly? | *The fallen branch was an impediment across the path. | I had an impediment for lunch. | The apple was an impediment in the pie. | The impediment sang a happy tune.
